From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Wed, 30 Nov 1994 12:51:55 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-536] BASICS:  Coalitions and firepower

>From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
>Date: Wed, 30 Nov 1994 00:58:50 -0800
>Subject: [#94-11-516] BASICS: Anarchy is not Chaos
>
>> [discussion on police, defense against criminals, etc.]
>
>  An occasional problem with defense by force of arms is the need to
>maintain enough firepower to cope with the largest coalition that can
>ever to take arms against you.

No; you merely need to establish mutual defense contracts with enough of
your neighbors such that an attack on you will provoke a response large
enough to serve as a deterrent.  Any individual may be an easy target, but
{individual plus neighbors} is just too big and nasty to attack.

The each-man-is-a-superpower strategy does become cost-effective, however,
if you have personal nukes (the Armadillo strategy in Vinge's "The
Ungoverned").

>Sounds like it would save us all lots
>of money if we got together and backed a police force that disbanded,
>disarmed and dispersed such coalitions, as they formed.

Too monopolistic.  Instead, join one of multiple competing coalitions.
Such bodies can not only engage in the mutual-protection web described
above, but they also have a financial incentive to act as judicial and
peace-making (diplomatic) agencies, since it's _their_ property and
personnel that are on the line if disagreements degenerate into armed
conflict.

Oh, make sure that your coalition isn't geographically-defined; otherwise
you're in danger of ending up with a state.

>Said police
>force would of course have to take action against any such coalition,
>even if the coalition's members had not agreed to the police force in
>the first place.

Under your system, what recourse does one have if this central force (let's
stop being coy and call it a state) starts going beyond its charter and
sticking its nose in where it doesn't belong -- say, bedrooms and
bloodstreams?

>  In any case, the issue is presumably one of economics; if the
>state-controlled police force is truly a more cost-effective solution
>to the problem of personal physical security than is arbitrarily large
>private ownership of weapons, then it will eventually drive the
>gun-toters out of the market.

We are presently engaged in a great political struggle to see if this is in
fact the case.  Since, as J. Neal Schulman has pointed out, 70 million
Americans own guns, I'm betting on "our" side (the side of individual
freedom).  Woe to the world if we lose!

>  We can use the funds reclaimed from the ammo budget for more
>important things, like research and development to hasten the arrival
>of post-humanist civilization.

Regrettably, the monopolistic police force (state) has demonstrated a
marked tendency to use its monopoly on violence to extract tribute from its
subjects far in excess of that needed for defense, and spend the booty on
all kinds of silly projects having little do with posthuman ambitions and
much to do with enriching the functionaries of the state and their cronies.

C'mon, Jay, you know better than this.
