From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Wed, 30 Nov 1994 13:10:25 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-539] BASICS: Anarchy is not Chaos

At 10:53 AM 11/30/94, Phil Goetz wrote:
>It sounds like the Dark Ages to me, when every big town had a watch,
>every village hoped their local toughs would be stronger than raiding parties,
>and being raped &/or murdered was a risk that was part of being a farmer
>far outside the city walls.

In AD 900, there were no firearms -- "the great equalizer".  An armed
family with a defensible home would be hard for any small raiding party to
dislodge -- and for larger raiding parties, there are mutual-protection
agreements with neighboring farms or the nearest town, or contract
protection with a commercial protection agency (they didn't have
telecommunications, remote sensing, or automated alarm systems in AD 900,
either).

SCENARIO:  Daisy Mae is out plowing the back 40 when she sees a handful of
strangers driving up on bikes.  They're clearly trespassing, so she shoots
first and asks questions later.

SCENARIO:  Daisy Mae is out plowing the back 40 when a neighbor radios that
he and some friends want to motorcycle through the Yokum pasture.  When
they ride by, Daisy Mae waves.

SCENARIO:  The East Podunk Motorcycle Raiders and Sewing Circle (55 members
on cross-country bikes) are headed for the Yokum farm.  They see the signs
saying "Armed response!  Joe's Protection Racket!  No Trespassing!"  They
wisely circle around the Yokum place.

SCENARIO:  Daisy Mae is out plowing the back 40 when a big motorcycle gang
roars through the fence.  She radios Joe's Protection Racket in Mount
Pilate, meanwhile opening up on the intruders with the tractor's guns.
Five minutes later, one of Joe's helicopter gunships, out on morning
patrol, circles over and blasts the shit out of everything not identifiably
Yokum property.

SCENARIO:  Under new management, Joe's Protection Racket doubles its prices
and decrees that all customers must henceforth refrain from smoking or
chewing tobacco.  Joe's customers switch to Pete's Private Police,
Freelance Sheriffs Inc., or any of Joe's other competitors, and meanwhile
open up on any of Joe's operatives that come within range.

Sources:  "The Machinery of Freedom" by David Friedman (this could be
considered the "anarchy FAQ" you asked about, I guess); and "The
Ungoverned" by Vernor Vinge (in the collections _True Names_ and _Across
Realtime_).

